<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swedish Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stormaktstiden, Age of Great Power</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swedish Empire refers to the period, known in Swedish as Stormaktstiden or the Age of Great Power, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> dominated the Baltic region in the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries. Built on military reform, Baltic conquests and gains from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, it controlled territories around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its power ended with defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swedish-empire.jpg" alt="Swedish Empire" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wheatley2 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Swedish_Empire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Great Power Era is usually dated from 1611, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> came to the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s rise rested on a rapidly expanding metals economy: by the early seventeenth century it was northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading exporter of iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, which made up nearly 63 per cent of its exports in 1637 and more than 80 per cent in 1685. The Crown worked the Stora Kopparberg copper mine at Falun, then the largest in Europe, to help finance the king's political and military ambitions.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and conquered about half of the member states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, though its defeat at the Battle of Nördlingen in 1634 damaged confidence in it among the German states it controlled. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> intervened on the same side, fortunes shifted again. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 gave Sweden territories in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>. Sweden also founded New Sweden on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> in 1638.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> died in 1632, high nobles such as the Oxenstierna family ruled as regents for long periods. The empire reached its height in the Northern War of 1655 to 1660. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658 Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, Trøndelag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, though under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-copenhagen-1660">Treaty of Copenhagen</a> of 1660 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> gave back Trøndelag and Bornholm. The Peace of Oliva of 1660 confirmed Sweden's possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, and the Treaty of Cardis in 1661 gave it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kexholm">Kexholm</a>. The empire then covered 1,100,000 square kilometres, with about 2,500,000 people.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a>, which began in 1675 after a defeat at Fehrbellin, exposed its weakness, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> was taken by its enemies. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of 1680 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI</a> recovered alienated crown lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> and forced the Treaty of Altranstädt in 1706, but was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709. He was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a> on 30 November 1718.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and with it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s time as a great power, though it remained a regional power until the nineteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1809, and Sweden's last continental territory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. The empire is also remembered as the leading power of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> for about ninety years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swedish Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Great Northern War, a Russian army under Admiral Fyodor Apraksin defeated the defending Finnish army of the <b>Swedish Empire</b> under General Carl Gustaf Armfeldt at Pälkäne, though Armfeldt withdrew his army in good order.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-17">17 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
